The '''BMC Wiyar''' is a {{w|mid-size car|mid-size}} {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}} manufactured by the [[Burawi Motor Company]] (BMC) since 1988. Its name derives from the [[Burawi language]] word for "young man." It was introduced as the successor to the [[BMC 20]] as part of BMC's "fleet of the future" programme, a comprehensive renewal of the company's product line following the adoption of yearly production cycles in 1985. The Wiyar has been in continuous production since its introduction, with incremental updates introduced each model year.

The Wiyar is one of BMC's most widely produced vehicles and serves as the standard {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}} in the state taxi fleet of [[Burawa]], with internal combustion engine (2016 model year) and electric (2018 model year) variants currently in service.

== Development ==

=== Background ===

Prior to 1985, the [[Burawi Motor Company]] released vehicles at an inconsistent rate, with new models introduced irregularly and model years often spanning several years without meaningful updates. In 1985, BMC adopted a yearly production cycle, committing to small incremental changes each model year rather than irregular major revisions. The shift was intended to provide predictable update schedules for fleet operators, standardise manufacturing processes, and introduce greater diversity across the company's product range.

The yearly production cycle formed the basis of the "fleet of the future" programme, a planned rollout of new and replacement vehicles across multiple body styles and market segments. The programme began with the [[BMC Hooyo]], a {{w|hatchback}} introduced in 1985 as the first vehicle launched under the new cycle, and continued with successive introductions through the late 1980s and early 1990s.

=== Introduction ===

The Wiyar was introduced in 1988 as a {{w|mid-size car|mid-size}} {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}}, replacing the [[BMC 20]] in BMC's sedan lineup. It was launched alongside the [[BMC Aabbe]], a {{w|station wagon}}, and the [[BMC Badalga]], a {{w|minibus}}, as part of the 1988 model year releases under the "fleet of the future" programme.

The Wiyar's design was intended to serve both the general consumer market and the state fleet market, including taxi service. Its mid-size dimensions made it suitable for urban driving conditions in Burawi cities while providing adequate passenger space for taxi use.

The Wiyar was part of BMC's "fleet of the future" programme, which introduced new vehicles across multiple segments to replace older models and expand the company's product diversity. Other vehicles launched under the programme, named after Burawi cities, include the [[BMC Badalga]] ({{w|minibus}}, 1988), [[BMC Khar]] ({{w|minivan}}, 1990), and [[BMC Jowhar]] ({{w|sport utility vehicle|light SUV}}, 1993).

== Model year evolution ==

Under the yearly production cycle adopted in 1985, the Wiyar receives incremental updates each model year. Changes are typically minor, encompassing trim revisions, mechanical adjustments, technology updates, and compliance modifications. Major redesigns or platform changes occur less frequently, with successive model years sharing the same fundamental architecture.

Notable model year variants include:

=== 2016 model year ===

The 2016 Wiyar is an internal combustion engine variant currently in service as a standard sedan in the [[Taxis of Burawa|state taxi fleet]]. It represents one of the last ICE-powered Wiyar variants produced before the [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]] mandated that all new public transit vehicles purchased by the state must be electric.

=== 2018 model year ===

The 2018 Wiyar is an electric variant introduced following the [[Smart Transportation Act of 2016]]. It is currently in service in the state taxi fleet alongside the 2016 ICE variant. The electric Wiyar was developed as part of BMC's broader fleet electrification programme, which also produced the electric [[BMC Badalga]] (2022 model year).

== Use in taxi service ==

The Wiyar has served as the standard {{w|sedan (automobile)|sedan}} in the [[Taxis of Burawa|state taxi fleet]] since its introduction, replacing earlier BMC sedan models designated under the [[Taxicab Standardization Bill of 1977]]. Under the five-year review cycle established by the Bill, the [[Ministry of Transportation (Burawa)|Ministry of Transportation]] periodically updates the list of approved taxi vehicles, and the Wiyar has been designated as the standard sedan for taxi service in successive review cycles since 1988.

Both the 2016 (ICE) and 2018 (electric) Wiyar variants are currently operated by metropolitan and urban administration transit agencies across Burawa. The vehicles are painted in the standardised {{w|lime (color)|lime}} green and white {{w|checkered pattern|checkered}} livery introduced in 1977.

